BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:khoj-mozilla-counterstructures-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Khoj x Mozilla Foundation: Counterstructures Residency 20
 26 (Delhi)
DESCRIPTION:Counterstructures is Mozilla Foundation's cultural R&D residenc
 y\, in which creative technologists prototype alternatives to dominant tec
 h systems: cultural artifacts\, installations and tools that question and 
 reframe them. Each edition operationalises the findings of a regional part
 icipatory assembly into thematic tracks\; the Delhi edition\, hosted by Kh
 oj International Artists' Association\, works from Mozilla's assemblies wi
 th G5A in Delhi and Mumbai and runs on two tracks. RETAINING CULTURAL AUTH
 ORITY asks what happens when oral\, embodied or community-held knowledge i
 s converted into datasets and machine-readable forms\, which cultural mate
 rials become visible to AI systems and which do not\, and how communities 
 can retain authority over what is digitised\, interpreted\, reused or deli
 berately kept private. REMEMBERING AND THINKING WITH MACHINES asks where t
 hought resides when people think with machines\, at what stage human judgm
 ent enters the system\, and what cultural or professional knowledge is los
 t when judgment is standardised through automation. Proposals may respond 
 to one or both tracks and can take many forms\, but must critically counte
 r the questions the tracks raise rather than simply illustrate them. WHAT 
 RESIDENTS DO: prototype an artifact\, installation\, system\, artwork or t
 ool for public exhibition\; take part in listening\, reading\, workshops a
 nd facilitated critique\, testing ideas with peers and community members\;
  write a 750-word report on the project's development and insights for the
  Counterstructures report and online archive\; and present the work public
 ly. WHO IT IS FOR: creative technologists and multidisciplinary artists us
 ing technology as a medium for cultural inquiry\, bringing critical perspe
 ctives and research-driven practice. Experience with AI can be early-stage
  or advanced\, but SOME PROFICIENCY WITH CODING IS REQUIRED. ELIGIBILITY: 
 this Delhi edition is open to Indian nationals only. Earlier editions ran 
 with tiat in San Francisco (responding to Hollywood's 8 Rules for AI) and 
 with Rhizome in New York\, whose residents showed at the New Museum. APPLY
  through the Mozilla Foundation form at https://mozillafoundation.tfaforms
 .net/f/khojresidency\; the guiding principles for the call are set out in 
 the form. Deadline 13 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Khoj International 
 Artists' Association in collaboration with Mozilla Foundation\nLocation: N
 ew Delhi\, India. Weekly in-person sessions at Khoj. INDIAN NATIONALS ONLY
 .\nAward: Mozilla's Creative Futures: Counterstructures programme publishe
 s USD 2\,500 per resident (USD 2\,000 stipend plus a USD 500 materials bud
 get)\, along with weekly in-person work sessions\, guest lectures from pra
 ctitioners across art\, technology and community design\, and publication 
 of the finished project in the collective documentation report. CAVEAT: ne
 ither the Khoj call page nor the Mozilla application form restates a figur
 e for this six-week Delhi edition\, and the published package covers the t
 en-week format\, so confirm the amount with Khoj before applying. No appli
 cation fee.\nDuration: Six weeks\, 1 October to 12 November 2026\, running
  weekly in Delhi\, with final projects presented publicly 13 to 15 Novembe
 r 2026.\n\nMore: https://khojstudios.org/opportunity/open-call-counterstru
 ctures-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://khojstudios.org/opportunity/open-call-counterstructures-residen
 cy/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New Delhi\, India. Weekly in-person sessions at Khoj. INDIAN NATIO
 NALS ONLY.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ctm-resynthesising-traditional-at-the-extreme-2027@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: CTM Festival: Resynthesising the Traditional 2027 Artisti
 c Lab (At the Extreme)
DESCRIPTION:Resynthesising the Traditional is CTM Festival's long-term init
 iative co-curated by Stas Sharifulla\, taking resynthesis (the computer-mu
 sic method of analysing a sound to extract its components) as a metaphor f
 or critically engaging with tradition and heritage\, against conservative 
 views of culture as frozen and untouchable. The artistic lab at its heart 
 is an open toolbox for artists connecting to the traditional outside exoti
 cisation\, cultural essentialism and uneven power dynamics. THEME\, AT THE
  EXTREME: this edition invites artists and practice-based researchers whos
 e work examines the ethical\, aesthetic and political implications of trad
 itions entangled with extremity: intensity\, transgression\, transformatio
 n\, marginality\, outsiderness\, endurance\, conflict and excess. The call
  explicitly looks past the usual noise\, violence and loud-popular-music t
 ropes and past masculine codings of the extreme\, toward current queer and
  feminist reframings\, treating extremity as a relational condition\, chos
 en or imposed\, produced between majoritarian centres and their minoritari
 an outsides. Guiding questions include what it means to follow a tradition
  to its extreme consequences\, who is allowed to push tradition to its lim
 its and whose practices get framed as inauthentic or dangerous\, what it m
 eans to practise tradition under war\, displacement\, repression or ecolog
 ical crisis\, and how traditions might offer ways of inhabiting extreme ti
 mes. Preference goes to work engaging technological\, bodily\, aesthetic a
 nd theoretical forms at that intersection\, especially in unexpected or ra
 rely recognised ways. WHAT HAPPENS: theory and practice workshops from the
  co-hosts with individual and group exercises\, closed 15 to 20 minute ora
 l presentations by each participant\, a solo public performance or present
 ation (15 to 20 minutes) at a free-entry event\, inputs from specialists s
 uch as artists\, researchers and technologists\, guided individual and gro
 up sonic improvisations\, field trips and site visits\, and feedback sessi
 ons with the co-hosts and curators. AFTERWARDS: participants may propose n
 ew artistic work or research\, solo or in collaborations formed at the lab
 \, to the artistic team\; selected works are shortlisted\, and up to two a
 re commissioned and presented at CTM in January/February 2028. ELIGIBILITY
 : an existing practice tied to (re)connecting with traditional ideas and s
 ounds through a contemporary lens and how that overlaps with the extreme\,
  plus clear ideas or questions about the next step you want to take. Appro
 aches may be technological\, instrument-focused\, compositional\, aestheti
 c\, research-oriented or otherwise. Working knowledge of English is recomm
 ended\, and any concerns about that can be flagged in the form. Up to six 
 participants are selected by co-hosts Karina Utomo (extreme metal vocalist
 \, composer and collaborative artist) and Luigi Monteanni (sound researche
 r and ethnomusicologist completing a PhD at SOAS on extreme metal in Bandu
 ng). The lab is a time-intensive full-day commitment and participants must
  agree to give the short public performance. APPLY through the form with a
  portfolio link (PDF\, max 10 pages\, public access\, works from the last 
 five years\, text kept minimal) and a link to a specific audio track or se
 ction (max five minutes) that best represents your current practice. CTM a
 sks that AI support rather than replace your own voice in the application.
  TIMELINE: deadline 28 September 2026\; participants announced mid to late
  October 2026. Questions to resynth@ctm-festival.de.\n\nOrganisation: CTM 
 Festival\, co-curated by Stas Sharifulla\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany. Open
  to artists worldwide\; solo artists and projects only.\nAward: Travel bud
 get of up to EUR 300 per person\, plus accommodation and catering for the 
 duration of the lab and a festival pass to CTM 2027 events. Visa fees are 
 supported where required\, and letters of invitation are provided for visa
  or funding applications. NO artist fee or stipend for the lab itself. Aft
 erwards\, up to two new works arising from the lab may be commissioned and
  presented at CTM Festival in January/February 2028. No application fee.\n
 Duration: Seven days: arrival 24 January\, lab 26 to 31 January 2027 (10:0
 0 to 19:00 daily)\, public presentations 26 January\, departure 1 February
  2027.\n\nMore: https://www.ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/resyn
 thesising-the-traditional-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/resynthesising-the
 -traditional-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany. Open to artists worldwide\; solo artists and pro
 jects only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sat-research-creation-residency-arts-technologies-2026-28@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261018
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:Deadline: SAT: Research-Creation Residency Program in Arts and Tech
 nologies 2026-28 (Montreal)
DESCRIPTION:The Societe des arts technologiques (SAT) in Montreal runs a re
 search-creation residency program in arts and technologies for artists and
  collectives working in the performing arts and/or media arts. The residen
 cy funds a research-creation stage of a project in technological arts rath
 er than the finalisation of a finished work\, so the call weighs the explo
 ration process and the innovation in it above a polished outcome. Each sel
 ected project receives a CAD 10\,000 grant\, whether the applicant is an i
 ndividual artist or a collective\, plus access to SAT spaces and equipment
 \, technical and creative support from the SAT teams\, mentoring and train
 ing activities\, and documentation and archiving. Access to spaces and equ
 ipment is assessed against the project's needs and granted subject to avai
 lability. Travel\, meal and accommodation costs beyond the grant are not c
 overed. Open to professional artists and artistic collectives worldwide\, 
 at any level of experience and from any primary discipline\, provided the 
 profile and project suit immersive technologies\; collectives must submit 
 a joint portfolio showing they can carry out the project independently\, p
 articularly the visual and sound creation inside a dome. Each application 
 must include a form and supporting documents setting out the concept\, the
  technical solutions considered\, and what will be explored during the res
 idency. One application per project and per person\; incomplete applicatio
 ns are not evaluated\; use permanent\, downloadable links rather than WeTr
 ansfer or similar temporary services. Accommodations and alternative suppo
 rt documents (large print and so on) are available on request at residence
 s@sat.qc.ca. Deadline 18 October 2026. Apply through the Google Form linke
 d from the SAT open-call page.\n\nOrganisation: Societe des arts technolog
 iques (SAT)\nLocation: Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Sa
 int-Laurent\, Montreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and col
 lectives from anywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.\n
 Award: CAD 10\,000 grant per selected project\, for individual artists and
  collectives alike. Also included: access to SAT workspaces during the res
 idency periods\, access to equipment and software resources subject to ava
 ilability\, mentoring and training activities (on site and remote)\, creat
 ive and technical support from the SAT teams\, and documentation and archi
 ving of the work. Travel\, meals and accommodation beyond the grant are NO
 T covered. No application fee.\nDuration: Two months\, extendable up to th
 ree months depending on the project\; residencies run across the 2026-28 p
 rogram.\n\nMore: https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-pr
 ogram-in-arts-technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-program-in-arts-
 technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
LOCATION:Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Saint-Laurent\, M
 ontreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and collectives from a
 nywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:video-pool-matr-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Video Pool: Media Art + Technology Residency (MATR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:Video Pool's Media Art + Technology Residency supports experime
 ntal\, independent\, technology-based work. The selected artist or artists
  develop a new project (or adapt and transform an existing one) in direct 
 response to the intimate scale and specific character of the Poolside Gall
 ery\, culminating in an exhibition there. The call is deliberately open on
  medium: video\, sound\, installation\, digital media\, performance\, inte
 ractive technologies\, expanded cinema and interdisciplinary practices are
  all welcome. VP asks proposals to speak clearly to your practice\, the qu
 estions\, materials or ideas that drive the work\, and how that work might
  grow\, shift or take new form inside the gallery. It is explicitly intere
 sted in expansive proposals\, work that pushes past the gallery's four wal
 ls to engage its surrounding spaces (hallways\, windows\, projection wall)
  or that reimagines what the gallery itself can hold\, and in projects tha
 t engage technology as a creative\, critical\, aesthetic or socio-politica
 l tool. Priority is given to artists from equity-seeking communities whose
  practices contribute to the advancement of experimental media arts. Resid
 ents get full access to VP's available equipment\, facilities and technica
 l resources throughout the residency\, and applicants are encouraged to re
 view the equipment and facilities list before proposing. ELIGIBILITY: open
  to VP members (this is a member submission call)\; no nationality restric
 tion is stated\, but the residency and exhibition are in person in Winnipe
 g. APPLICATION: artist bio (1\,500 characters)\, headshot\, description of
  your artistic practice (3\,000 characters)\, project proposal for MATR (3
 \,000 characters)\, equipment and facility needs (750 characters)\, descri
 ption of how you would use the space (750 characters)\, CV\, up to five im
 ages (optional) and up to two video or documentation links. Submit via the
  online form at videopool.org/matr-2027-application/ by 30 August 2026\; a
 lternative submission formats can be arranged by contacting Emma (emma@vid
 eopool.org) or Iyunade (iyunade@videopool.org). General enquiries: info@vi
 deopool.org\, 204-949-9134.\n\nOrganisation: Video Pool Media Arts Centre\
 nLocation: In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gall
 ery\, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.\nAward
 : CAD 2\,250 artist fee\, covering both the residency period and the subse
 quent Poolside Gallery exhibition. Residents also get full access to VP's 
 equipment\, facilities and technical resources\; the Poolside Gallery itse
 lf is equipped with two projectors and four speakers. Applicants must be V
 P members\, so a membership is purchased separately\, but there is no sepa
 rate application fee.\nDuration: Up to two months to develop and install t
 he project\, including one month of dedicated access to the Poolside Galle
 ry. Residency takes place in Winter 2027\; exhibition dates TBD.\n\nMore: 
 https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-applic
 ations/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-app
 lications/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gallery
 \, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ars-biologica-artxscience-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ars Biologica ArtXScience Residency 2027-2028 (Ars Electr
 onica x Ceske Budejovice ECoC 2028)
DESCRIPTION:A fully funded art-science residency from Ars Electronica and C
 eske Budejovice\, European Capital of Culture 2028\, exploring how art and
  science can collaborate on urgent environmental challenges: climate emerg
 ence\, sustainable agriculture\, geoengineering\, permaculture\, water res
 ource management\, and the broader ecological impacts on local communities
 . Selected residents work in direct collaboration with local scientists in
  Ceske Budejovice. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, designers\, creatives and arti
 st collectives with more than 5 years of professional creative practice an
 d documented interdisciplinary work\; experience in community engagement a
 nd science collaborations is essential\; applicants must be 18+\, of any n
 ationality\, and legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. WHAT YOU G
 ET: a EUR 24\,000 package (EUR 6\,000 artist fee\, up to EUR 15\,000 produ
 ction\, up to EUR 3\,000 travel and subsistence) plus one month of housing
  covered separately\; two winners are selected. STRUCTURE: the residency r
 uns January 2027 to April 2028\, with a required on-site stay of up to one
  month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/September 2027\
 , and a March 2028 stay for festival installation\, exhibition and deinsta
 llation. HOW TO APPLY: online form with project title\, a written proposal
  of max 250 words\, a scientific direction statement (max 100 words)\, art
 istic vision (max 100 words)\, community connection plan (max 100 words)\,
  a super short pitch (3 to 30 words)\, 3 to 7 images\, an optional 1-minut
 e video\, a portfolio link and contact information. Two-round selection (o
 nline application then interview)\; winners notified by November 2026. Dea
 dline 28 September 2026 at midnight CET.\n\nOrganisation: Ars Electronica 
 and Ceske Budejovice - European Capital of Culture 2028\nLocation: Ceske B
 udejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any nationality aged 18+
  who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Required on-site s
 tays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/
 September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibition and deinstal
 lation.\nAward: EUR 24\,000 total package per winner: EUR 6\,000 artist fe
 e\, up to EUR 15\,000 production budget\, and up to EUR 3\,000 for travel\
 , accommodation and subsistence\, plus a separate budget covering one mont
 h of housing in Ceske Budejovice. Two positions available. Free to apply.\
 nDuration: January 2027 to April 2028\, with on-site blocks in early 2027 
 and March 2028\n\nMore: https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars
 -biologica-art-science-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars-biologica-art-scienc
 e-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ceske Budejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any natio
 nality aged 18+ who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Req
 uired on-site stays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional 
 stay in August/September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibiti
 on and deinstallation.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:latitude-air-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: LATITUDE Artist in Residence 2027 (Chicago)
DESCRIPTION:The LATITUDE Artist in Residence program (started in 2013) is a
  one-month production residency where 8-10 artists are granted full access
  to LATITUDE's facilities and community to develop their practice. Its foc
 us is supporting artists who want to use LATITUDE's printing and scanning 
 services to expand their practice\, regardless of medium or experience\; p
 ast residents include photographers\, painters\, performance artists\, new
  media artists\, and sculptors. WHAT RESIDENTS RECEIVE: a $1\,000 printing
  stipend\, unlimited scanning\, a personal studio with a calibrated workst
 ation and 24/7 access\, training and guidance on tech from staff\, an orga
 nised public event to present new work\, and lifetime free lab access. LAT
 ITUDE does not offer a housing stipend but will help schedule studio visit
 s during the month. PUBLIC PROGRAMMING: during the residency each artist p
 uts on one community programming event (past examples include panel discus
 sions\, artist talks\, performances\, workshops\, and critiques\; a galler
 y space is available for curation) and is asked to act as a mentor to the 
 community\, sharing insights and creative methods. WHO SHOULD APPLY: artis
 ts interested in production\, education\, and tech experimentation\; no pr
 ior printing experience\, art education\, or media specificity is required
 \, though professionalism and communication are expected. 2027 PANEL COMMI
 TTEE: Yechen Zhao\, B. Ingrid Olson\, Teri Carson. TIMELINE: applications 
 open 1 August 2026 (free before 16 August\, a $20 donation thereafter) and
  close 1 September 2026 at 12pm (noon) CST\; the selection committee revie
 ws in September\, meets to decide finalists in October\, and acceptances a
 re sent early November 2026. Apply at latitudechicago.org/artist-in-reside
 nce.\n\nOrganisation: LATITUDE Chicago\nLocation: Chicago\, Illinois\, USA
 . No housing stipend offered.\nAward: One-month production residency for 8
 -10 artists: a $1\,000 printing stipend\, unlimited scanning\, a personal 
 studio with a calibrated workstation\, staff training and guidance on tech
 \, an organised public event\, and lifetime free lab access. No housing st
 ipend. Free to apply before 16 August 2026\; a $20 donation thereafter.\nD
 uration: One month (residency takes place in 2027)\n\nMore: https://www.la
 titudechicago.org/artist-in-residence?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.latitudechicago.org/artist-in-residence?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Chicago\, Illinois\, USA. No housing stipend offered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:padimai-onchain-artist-residency-oops-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Padimai: OOPS Onchain Artist Residency 2026
DESCRIPTION:OOPS (Origin Of Primary Source) is the first edition of Padimai
  Art and Tech Studio's Onchain Artist Residency\, a three-month remote res
 idency inviting art practitioners to critically and creatively engage with
  NFTs as artistic registers and as systems of knowledge\, documentation\, 
 and access. Five practitioners are selected. SUPPORT: SGD 15\,000 honorari
 um per artist\, plus SGD 5\,000 per artist for documentation. The residenc
 y is remote\, with community engagement designed through online meetings\;
  at the end\, participants deliver three NFTs developed during the residen
 cy to Padimai. ELIGIBILITY: individual creative practitioners working at t
 he intersection of art\, technology\, and blockchain\; open internationall
 y. RESIDENCY: 20 September to 20 December 2026. DEADLINE: 15 August 2026. 
 Apply via the residency page.\n\nOrganisation: Padimai Art and Tech Studio
 \nLocation: Remote residency (Padimai is based in Singapore)\; open intern
 ationally.\nAward: SGD 15\,000 honorarium per artist plus an additional SG
 D 5\,000 per artist for documentation (audio\, video\, photography). Five 
 practitioners selected. No application fee.\nDuration: 3 months (20 Septem
 ber to 20 December 2026)\n\nMore: https://padimai.net/onchain-artist-resid
 ency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://padimai.net/onchain-artist-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote residency (Padimai is based in Singapore)\; open internatio
 nally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:icci-art-valley-international-visiting-art-scholars-2026@artificialnouv
 eau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260714
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260715
SUMMARY:Deadline: ICCI ART VALLEY: International Visiting Art Scholars 2026
DESCRIPTION:International visiting artist/scholar residency at the USC-SJTU
  Institute of Cultural and Creative Industry in Shanghai\, recruiting 5-6 
 participants for a cross-media\, cross-cultural and cross-spatial explorat
 ion of contemporary art at a time when AI and spatial computing are reshap
 ing artistic expression. Three program directions: Art and Technology (dig
 ital and material interaction)\; Humanistic Dialogue (cross-cultural under
 standing through exchange)\; and Urban Engagement (responding to urban tra
 nsformation). Residents receive an individual studio plus access to cerami
 cs\, computer lab\, sculpture\, sound\, VR and print facilities\, a librar
 y and gallery\, and private apartment housing (no meals). Support includes
  1-2 assistants\, organized museum and gallery visits\, exchange with loca
 l Chinese artists\, a group exhibition\, and media promotion. ELIGIBILITY:
  teaching or public-lecture/seminar experience at a university or art inst
 itution\; an active artistic practice (priority to interdisciplinary media
 \, digital art\, easel painting or integrated media)\; ability to organize
  1-2 public events (lectures or workshops) during the residency\; fluent E
 nglish presentation and strong cross-cultural communication skills. Select
 ed artists must donate two artworks for SJTU's permanent collection (provi
 de a list of at least five works)\, actively participate in ICCI activitie
 s\, and fully commit to the residency period. Selection announced July 30\
 , 2026. Working languages: English and Mandarin.\n\nOrganisation: USC-SJTU
  Institute of Cultural and Creative Industry (ICCI)\, Shanghai Jiao Tong U
 niversity\nLocation: Shanghai\, China\nAward: Minimum RMB 8\,000 one-time 
 project grant (for production and lecture/workshop materials)\, plus round
 -trip economy airfare from the artist's departure city\, accommodation\, a
 nd group-exhibition installation costs. Note: a one-time RMB 8\,000 progra
 m fee applies. Requires donating two artworks to Shanghai Jiao Tong Univer
 sity's permanent collection.\nDuration: Residency November 1 to December 1
 5\, 2026 (full commitment required)\n\nMore: https://icci.sjtu.edu.cn/en/e
 vent/view/90?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://icci.sjtu.edu.cn/en/event/view/90?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Shanghai\, China
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kb-researcher-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260927
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260928
SUMMARY:Deadline: KB Researcher-in-Residence (RiR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:ELIGIBILITY: two Researcher-in-Residence positions in 2027. Ope
 n to early-career researchers of any discipline (data science\, social sci
 ence\, computer science\, humanities) who are currently working on a PhD o
 r within 5 years of their PhD end date. You must have\, or be able to secu
 re\, an affiliation with a Dutch university or research institute willing 
 to second you\; external PhD students may use the funding to obtain such a
  contract. Your project must make use of one or more of the KB digital col
 lections and you must be able to work with primarily Dutch data. You must 
 be available at 0.5 FTE over 6 months and able to spend two days a week on
 -site at the KB in The Hague. COLLECTIONS: over 130 million digitized page
 s of newspapers\, magazines\, radio bulletins and books from 1470 onward (
 via Delpher and DBNL)\, a born-digital web collection of more than 23\,000
  websites\, ground-truth and derived datasets on the KB Lab\, and metadata
  including Linked Data. APPLY: use the official proposal template and subm
 it a single PDF by 27 September 2026 to dh@kb.nl\, after reading the call'
 s terms and conditions\; questions to dh@kb.nl. TIMELINE: deadline 27 Sept
 ember 2026\; internal eligibility review followed by an external committee
  review\; outcome notified November 2026\; residencies start Q1 and Q3 202
 7. DISCIPLINES: digital humanities / data science / computational research
  with cultural-heritage collections. NOTE: the output is usually a dataset
  or tool prototype hosted on the KB Lab\; you agree to share results via b
 log posts on the KB Lab and to publish resulting tools or data under an op
 en license within 6 months of the residency. Proposals should align with t
 he KB Research Agenda or the Cultural AI Lab.\n\nOrganisation: KB\, Nation
 al Library of the Netherlands\nLocation: The Hague\, Netherlands (two days
  per week on-site at the KB premises)\nAward: Secondment at 0.5 FTE for 6 
 months based on your current salary\, up to EUR 5\,590 gross per month for
  a full-time-equivalent position\, plus travel expenses\, office facilitie
 s\, and support from a research software engineer\, research advisor\, and
  collection and data specialists. No application fee.\nDuration: 6 months 
 at 0.5 FTE (two days per week on-site)\; two positions in 2027\, starting 
 Q1 and Q3\n\nMore: https://lab.kb.nl/news/call-kb-researcher-residence-rir
 -2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://lab.kb.nl/news/call-kb-researcher-residence-rir-2027?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:The Hague\, Netherlands (two days per week on-site at the KB premi
 ses)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:transmediale-pro-helvetia-digital-art-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: transmediale x Pro Helvetia Digital Art Residency 2027
DESCRIPTION:The fifth iteration of transmediale's Digital Art Residency\, r
 un with the Swiss Arts Council Pro Helvetia\, awarded to one individual or
  collective working in digital art practices at the intersection of media 
 arts\, performance\, sound and moving image. THEME: the 2027 programme sit
 s under transmediale's 40th-anniversary framework RFC 40\, named after the
  early internet Request for Comments series\, and treats the festival as a
  counter-infrastructure: the call asks how a network stays legible to itse
 lf while surrounding structures break down\, and how technical paradigms c
 an be reconfigured into new cultural protocols outside extractive logics\,
  moving from critique to construction. Proposals should offer alternatives
  to how we understand\, respond to and shape the technological conditions 
 we inhabit. WHAT IS OFFERED: an artist fee of CHF 2\,000 per month for two
  months\; a CHF 15\,000 production grant supporting a new work or the cont
 inuation of an existing project\, open to all formats and building on the 
 research begun during the residency (the project must be completed by Dece
 mber 2027 at the latest)\; travel and accommodation for the festival and t
 he residency\; a shared modular working space at transmediale studio\; con
 nections to Berlin institutions and stakeholders\; a public presentation i
 n transmediale's year-round studio programme\; and the option of an online
  publication in transmediale's journal. STRUCTURE: kick-off with a present
 ation of the residency projects during transmediale 2027 (26 to 31 January
  2027\, attendance optional but strongly recommended)\; onboarding and the
  first hybrid stretch in May 2027\; the mandatory on-site month in Berlin 
 in June 2027\; and a second hybrid stretch including reporting in July 202
 7. Availability for the festival week and for the whole of June in Berlin 
 are treated as key prerequisites. TIMELINE: open call 16 July to 28 August
  2026 (23:59 CEST)\; selected residents announced October 2026. Applicatio
 ns are only accepted via transmediale's submission forms. Contact: info[at
 ]transmediale.de.\n\nOrganisation: transmediale (in cooperation with the S
 wiss Arts Council Pro Helvetia)\nLocation: Hybrid: remote plus on-site at 
 transmediale studio\, silent green Kulturquartier\, Berlin-Wedding\, Germa
 ny. RESTRICTED: applicants must hold Swiss nationality or permanent reside
 nce in Switzerland and be firmly established in the Swiss cultural scene (
 Pro Helvetia's general requirements apply).\nAward: Artist fee of CHF 2\,0
 00 per month across the two-month residency (CHF 4\,000)\, plus a producti
 on grant of CHF 15\,000 for the selected project\, plus travel and accommo
 dation for both the festival and the residency month. Duos and collectives
  may apply but the fee and the production grant stay the same and must be 
 shared. No application fee.\nDuration: Two-month hybrid residency between 
 mid-May and mid-July 2027\, with the whole of June 2027 on-site in Berlin 
 (mandatory)\n\nMore: https://transmediale.de/en/news/digital-art-residency
 -2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://transmediale.de/en/news/digital-art-residency-2027?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hybrid: remote plus on-site at transmediale studio\, silent green 
 Kulturquartier\, Berlin-Wedding\, Germany. RESTRICTED: applicants must hol
 d Swiss nationality or permanent residence in Switzerland and be firmly es
 tablished in the Swiss cultural scene (Pro Helvetia's general requirements
  apply).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:art-explora-cite-internationale-des-arts-2027@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261106
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261107
SUMMARY:Deadline: Art Explora x Cite internationale des arts Residency Prog
 ramme\, 2027 Sessions
DESCRIPTION:Residency programme run by the Art Explora Foundation with the 
 Cite internationale des arts at its Montmartre site\, supporting work that
  engages scientific and technological exploration and the major social and
  environmental issues of the present. Residents receive a studio-apartment
  of around 50 sqm\, a monthly living grant of EUR 1\,000\, a production gr
 ant of up to EUR 3\,000\, and a round-trip travel grant\, plus access to p
 roduction facilities and local networks. Two tracks are offered: a SOLO pr
 ogramme for artists and writers across visual arts\, performance\, digital
  practices\, literary and other experimental disciplines\, and a COLLECTIV
 E programme for duos or collectives working collaboratively on interdiscip
 linary\, experimental or research-based projects\, for which a single stud
 io-apartment designed for collaborative work hosts two residents sharing a
  bed\, and only one member should submit the application. Eligible artisti
 c disciplines are visual arts\, performance and live arts\, digital arts\,
  curatorial practices\, and writing and literary practices\; eligible rese
 arch fields are humanities\, social sciences\, cultural studies and histor
 y\, technological innovation\, and ecology. No output is required at the e
 nd of the residency\, but participation in the Open Studios is mandatory. 
 ELIGIBILITY: artists\, artist collectives\, researchers\, curators and wri
 ters of all nationalities\, no age restriction\, with a minimum of five ye
 ars of professional activity and works or research exhibited at national o
 r international level. The Cite lists 20 to 22 residencies per year on thi
 s programme. Deadline 6 November 2026\, 23:59 Europe/Paris. Questions: res
 idences@artexplora.org. FLAG: the grant figures above come from the open-c
 all text supplied to the desk\; neither the Cite nor Art Explora publishes
  amounts on the programme page\, so confirm them\, along with the exact de
 adline\, before applying.\n\nOrganisation: Art Explora Foundation with the
  Cite internationale des arts\nLocation: Paris\, France (Cite internationa
 le des arts\, Montmartre site\, 24 rue Norvins\, 75018). Open to applicant
 s of all nationalities with no age restriction.\nAward: Monthly living gra
 nt of EUR 1\,000\, production grant of up to EUR 3\,000\, a round-trip tra
 vel grant\, and a fully equipped studio-apartment of roughly 50 sqm. For c
 ollectives and duos the living grant (up to EUR 2\,000) and the production
  grant (up to EUR 3\,000) are shared between members. Access to production
  facilities and local networks. No application fee.\nDuration: 3 to 6 mont
 hs. Session 1: March to August 2027. Session 2: September 2027 to February
  2028.\n\nMore: https://www.citeinternationaledesarts.fr/en/programme-de-r
 esidence/art-explora-x-cite-internationale-des-arts/?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.citeinternationaledesarts.fr/en/programme-de-residence/art-
 explora-x-cite-internationale-des-arts/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Paris\, France (Cite internationale des arts\, Montmartre site\, 2
 4 rue Norvins\, 75018). Open to applicants of all nationalities with no ag
 e restriction.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:k3-tanzplan-hamburg-choreography-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: K3 | Tanzplan Hamburg: Choreography Residency 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:K3 in Hamburg is one of the better-funded choreography residenc
 ies in Europe\, and it is aimed squarely at people who want research time 
 rather than a production deadline. Three choreographers are selected for e
 ight months at Kampnagel with a monthly scholarship\, studio access\, a pr
 oduction budget\, and dramaturgical\, technical and PR support\, plus outr
 each work and a public presentation at the end. Selection runs in two stag
 es. ELIGIBILITY: individual choreographers at an emerging to early mid-car
 eer stage who have already produced independent choreographic work outside
  an educational context. Open internationally\; formal dance training is n
 ot required. Teams and ensembles are not eligible\, only individuals. Appl
 ications close 12 August 2026 at 16:00 CEST.\n\nOrganisation: K3 | Tanzpla
 n Hamburg (Kampnagel)\nLocation: Hamburg\, Germany. Open internationally.\
 nAward: Monthly scholarship (EUR 2\,500 per month in the 2025/26 cycle\; t
 he 2027/28 figure had not been announced at the time of listing)\, plus on
 e-time travel expenses\, visa assistance\, a moderate production budget\, 
 studio access\, and dramaturgical\, technical and PR support. No applicati
 on fee is stated.\nDuration: Eight months\, August 2027 to April 2028\n\nM
 ore: https://www.k3-hamburg.de/en/k3/jobs&callforproposals?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.k3-hamburg.de/en/k3/jobs&callforproposals?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hamburg\, Germany. Open internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:drift-choreographer-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: DRIFT: Choreographer Open Call 2026-27 (Dance and Researc
 h In The Future Time)
DESCRIPTION:DRIFT (Dance and Research In The Future Time) is a four-year Cr
 eative Europe cooperation between ten European organisations\, led by Thea
 tre de Liege and Maison de la danse. The distinguishing feature is that it
  funds artistic research rather than production: you are expected to arriv
 e with questions\, not a finished piece you already intend to make\, and r
 eviewers read every section of the application through that lens. STRUCTUR
 E: each of the ten partner organisations selects one choreographer from it
 s own country\, forming a cohort of ten. Over the programme you get an Onb
 oarding Lab\, two weeks of residency with your national partner\, three co
 llaborative Online Labs\, a Festival Lab in Tunis\, individual artistic an
 d curatorial mentoring\, and a work-in-progress showcase at the Biennale d
 e la danse in Lyon\, all backed by a EUR 20\,000 research budget that cove
 rs your fees\, travel\, accommodation and any collaborators. SECOND PHASE:
  after a further evaluation\, five of the ten artists may receive co-produ
 ction support and touring across the DRIFT network. That phase is separate
  from this application and is not guaranteed. ELIGIBILITY: professional ch
 oreographers in contemporary dance or performance\, at a mid-career stage\
 , meaning an established national practice but not yet fully established i
 nternationally. You must be based in Belgium\, France\, Germany (Berlin re
 gion only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Italy\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tunisia or Portug
 al\, and you apply through the partner organisation representing your coun
 try of residence\, not centrally. Good English and availability for all pr
 ogramme dates are required. The country list is strict\, so check it befor
 e you start. TIMELINE: applications opened 1 July 2026 and close 31 August
  2026\; selection through September\; results 9 October 2026\; programme r
 uns November 2026 to September 2027.\n\nOrganisation: DRIFT\, a four-year 
 Creative Europe cooperation of ten organisations led by Theatre de Liege a
 nd Maison de la danse\nLocation: Activities across Europe: kick-off in Ber
 lin\, Festival Lab in Tunis\, showcase in Lyon\, online labs\, plus a resi
 dency hosted by your own national DRIFT partner. Applicants must be based 
 in Belgium\, France\, Germany (Berlin region only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Ita
 ly\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tunisia or Portugal\, and apply through the partner
  organisation for their country of residence.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 research
  budget per choreographer\, covering artistic fees\, travel\, accommodatio
 n and optional collaborator costs. Also included: an Onboarding Lab\, two 
 weeks of residency with your national DRIFT partner\, three collaborative 
 Online Labs\, a Festival Lab in Tunis\, individual artistic mentoring and 
 individual curatorial mentoring\, and a work-in-progress presentation at t
 he Biennale de la danse in Lyon. No application fee.\nDuration: November 2
 026 to September 2027. Kick-off in Berlin 30 November to 1 December 2026\;
  Festival Lab in Tunis 18-21 March 2027\; Biennale de la danse showcase in
  Lyon 18-25 September 2027.\n\nMore: https://driftdance.eu/artists/open-ca
 ll?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://driftdance.eu/artists/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Activities across Europe: kick-off in Berlin\, Festival Lab in Tun
 is\, showcase in Lyon\, online labs\, plus a residency hosted by your own 
 national DRIFT partner. Applicants must be based in Belgium\, France\, Ger
 many (Berlin region only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Italy\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tu
 nisia or Portugal\, and apply through the partner organisation for their c
 ountry of residence.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:scuola-piccola-zattere-international-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
SUMMARY:Deadline: Scuola Piccola Zattere: International Fellowship and Resi
 dency 2027 (Venice)
DESCRIPTION:Scuola Piccola Zattere is an independent non-profit centre for 
 continuous education and research in the expanded field of contemporary ar
 ts\, running exhibitions\, commissions\, fellowships\, workshops\, seminar
 s\, performances and public discussions as one connected research ecosyste
 m rather than separate strands. Two international fellowships are offered.
  The support is unusually solid for a research fellowship: EUR 5\,000 as a
  grant\, EUR 2\,000 as a research budget\, accommodation\, travel and a st
 udy space for three months. SEASON THEME: the 2026-2027 cycle is titled Li
 ving Document\, investigating archives\, regulatory documents\, collective
  rituals\, games\, performative instructions\, and the relationship betwee
 n bodies\, architecture and social organisation. Fellows join that shared 
 inquiry while continuing their own work\, and are expected to keep an acti
 ve presence in the institution and contribute to public events through wor
 kshops\, seminars\, presentations\, screenings or performances. The fellow
 ship overlaps with the run-up to and opening of the 2027 Architecture Bien
 nale\, which is a real advantage if your research benefits from that traff
 ic. ELIGIBILITY: artists\, curators\, researchers\, architects\, choreogra
 phers\, performers and writers of any nationality\, as individuals\, colle
 ctives or duos. Good English is required\, and non-EU applicants need a va
 lid passport for international travel. No age limit\, but the organisers s
 ay it is especially intended for practitioners in the post-academic stage 
 of their career\, typically under 40. You must be prepared to spend the fu
 ll period in Venice\, so this is not a remote or partial-attendance fellow
 ship. WHAT THEY WANT: an active research process that can develop through 
 discussion and experimentation\, tied to the Living Document theme\, not a
  finished stand-alone project proposal. The motivation letter\, portfolio 
 and public programming proposal are read as one integrated application. De
 adline 4 September 2026 at 10:00 CEST.\n\nOrganisation: Scuola Piccola Zat
 tere\nLocation: Venice\, Italy. Open to any nationality.\nAward: EUR 5\,00
 0 fellowship grant plus a EUR 2\,000 research budget\, accommodation in Ve
 nice\, an individual study space within a shared studio\, and round-trip t
 ravel to Venice. Collectives and duos share a single grant and one accommo
 dation. No application fee.\nDuration: Three months\, in one of two slots:
  early January to early April 2027\, or mid-April to mid-July 2027. Applic
 ants may name one slot or indicate availability for both.\n\nMore: https:/
 /scuolapiccolazattere.com/en/events/internationalfellowship-2-3?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://scuolapiccolazattere.com/en/events/internationalfellowship-2-3?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Venice\, Italy. Open to any nationality.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:titanik-mcult-residing-non-linear-technologies-sonic-worldings-2027@art
 ificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Residing Non-linear Technologies and Sonic Worldings (M-C
 ult x Titanik\, Helsinki and Turku)
DESCRIPTION:A new pilot residency from Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult 
 in Helsinki and Arte artists' association / Titanik gallery in Turku\, sit
 ting at the intersection of art\, media and sonic practice\, funded by the
  Nordic-Baltic Mobility Programme for Culture from Nordic Culture Point. T
 WO ARTISTS will be selected. THE PREMISE: the programme treats technology 
 as a situated practice and deliberately shifts attention away from the pro
 gress narratives promoted by large global technology companies\, toward lo
 cal\, indigenous and community-oriented perspectives. It focuses on making
  vernacular and non-linear technological and sonic environments visible\, 
 foregrounding vibrant local histories and traditions across the Nordic and
  Baltic region\, and asks what non-linear sonic and media practice might l
 ook like in the current social\, political and cultural landscape. WHAT EA
 CH ARTIST RECEIVES: EUR 3\,500 artist fee\; EUR 2\,000 production budget\;
  accommodation for two months divided between Helsinki and Turku\, one mon
 th in each\; travel costs for one return ticket from a Nordic-Baltic count
 ry\; access to Titanik's residency studio space and a working desk at M-Cu
 lt's office during each stay\; mentoring and curatorial support from both 
 organisations' staff\; and networking into the Finnish media art and conte
 mporary art scenes. In return the artist develops in-depth or focused rese
 arch and presents their practice and research at two public events\, one i
 n each city. WHO CAN APPLY: individual artists only\, explicitly NOT colle
 ctives this round\; artists from or based in Finland\, Sweden\, Norway\, D
 enmark\, Iceland\, Aland\, the Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Estonia\, Latvi
 a or Lithuania\; working in sound\, media and/or digital arts\, or the exp
 anded field of vernacular technologies\, all defined broadly by the organi
 sers. Applicants must be available to travel to Finland for the full two-m
 onth period across February and March 2027. The organisers welcome applica
 nts at all career stages and from all backgrounds. One submission per arti
 st: if two are sent\, only the later one is reviewed. HOW TO APPLY: submit
  via the form linked from the open call page with a brief letter of motiva
 tion including a research plan for the residency\, how it connects with th
 e programme's themes and what you would focus on (max 1-2 pages)\, your CV
 \, and a link to your portfolio. Applications in English only. DEADLINE: 3
 0 August 2026 at 23:59 UTC+3. Online interviews with shortlisted artists t
 ake place before 19 September 2026\, and all applicants are notified by em
 ail by the end of September 2026. JURY: Jussi Koitela\, Director of M-Cult
 \, Helsinki\, and Farbod Fakharzadeh\, Director of Arte ry and Titanik\, T
 urku. ACCOMMODATION AND ACCESSIBILITY: in Helsinki\, the Vallila Residency
  hosted by M-Cult is a 30 sqm apartment in a small wooden house with one r
 oom\, kitchen and bathroom with shower\; it is NOT fully accessible\, sitt
 ing on the first floor up stairs with no elevator (details from info@m-cul
 t.org). In Turku\, the Titanik A.i.R. apartment is 27 sqm on the top floor
  of a building with an elevator\, but the space including the bathroom is 
 not fully accessible (details from info@titanik.fi). A partner\, friend or
  child may join\, but their expenses are not covered. THE PARTNERS: M-Cult
 \, founded in 2000\, develops and presents art and media culture with a fo
 cus on the social and political dimensions of media and technology\, worki
 ng through art commissions\, residencies and learning programmes. Titanik\
 , established in Turku in 1988 by the artist-run organization Arte Ry\, ha
 s since 2006 run Titanik A.i.R.\, the first artist residency in Finland wi
 th a focus on sound art and sonic practices. CONTACT: Jussi Koitela (jussi
 .koitela@m-cult.org) or Farbod Fakharzadeh (director@arte.fi).\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult (Helsinki) and Arte artists' ass
 ociation / Titanik gallery (Turku)\nLocation: Finland\; one month in Helsi
 nki and one month in Turku. Open to artists from or based in Nordic and Ba
 ltic countries.\nAward: EUR 3\,500 artist fee plus EUR 2\,000 production b
 udget\, two months' accommodation split between Helsinki and Turku\, and o
 ne return travel ticket from a Nordic-Baltic country. No application fee s
 tated.\nDuration: Two months\, February to March 2027 (one month in each c
 ity)\n\nMore: https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-line
 ar-technologies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-linear-technolo
 gies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
LOCATION:Finland\; one month in Helsinki and one month in Turku. Open to ar
 tists from or based in Nordic and Baltic countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kluge-fellowships-library-of-congress-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kluge Fellowships at the Library of Congress 2027
DESCRIPTION:The John W. Kluge Center awards twelve residential fellowships 
 a year for humanistic and social science research that draws on the Librar
 y of Congress collections\, with interdisciplinary and cross-cultural proj
 ects particularly welcome. Fellows get four to eleven months in residence 
 at a stipend of USD 5\,000 per month\, work and discussion space in the Je
 fferson Building\, and access to the Library's specialist staff\, the worl
 d's largest law library\, extensive multilingual books and periodicals\, a
 nd deep special collections of manuscripts\, maps\, music\, films\, record
 ed sound\, prints and photographs\, alongside the wider scholarly communit
 y in Washington. The Center was established in 2000 through a USD 60 milli
 on endowment from John W. Kluge. ELIGIBILITY: scholars who received a term
 inal advanced degree (the highest degree available in the discipline\, usu
 ally a PhD\, JD or international equivalent) within the past seven years\,
  in the humanities\, social sciences\, or a professional field such as arc
 hitecture or law. The degree must be in hand at the time of submission\, a
 nd full-time students are therefore out of scope. Open to U.S. citizens an
 d foreign nationals\; selected foreign nationals are assisted in obtaining
  a Library-sponsored J-1 visa\, though holders of H-1B visas are not eligi
 ble. APPLICATION: through the Library's online portal\, opening 15 April 2
 026 and closing 11:59pm on 15 September 2026. Materials required are an ap
 plication form in English\, a CV (max 2 pages)\, a 500-word research abstr
 act\, a one-page statement on how the Library's collections will serve the
  work\, a project proposal with work plan (max 3 pages)\, a bibliography (
 max 3 pages)\, and two reference letters from referees who have read the p
 roposal. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: John W. Kluge Center\, Library of Congre
 ss\nLocation: Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas 
 Jefferson Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any n
 ationality: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange 
 visitor visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.\nAward: Stipend o
 f USD 5\,000 per month for a residency of four to eleven months\, so USD 2
 0\,000 to USD 55\,000 depending on the tenure awarded. Twelve Kluge Fellow
 ships are awarded each year. Stipends are paid monthly by electronic trans
 fer to a U.S. bank account. No application fee\, no participation fee.\nDu
 ration: Four to eleven months in residence\; fellows may take up residence
  at any point in the 18-month window after receiving the fellowship letter
 \n\nMore: https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowsh
 ips/fellowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowships/fel
 lowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas Jeffer
 son Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any nationa
 lity: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange visito
 r visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:platform-dali-residencies-2027-what-is-real@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Platform Dali Open Call for Residencies 2027: What is rea
 l?
DESCRIPTION:Platform Dali launches its first annual Open Call for Residenci
 es as part of its Research and Creation Programme\, with the inaugural 202
 7 edition built around the question 'What is real?'. Artists of any nation
 ality and any discipline are invited to propose a research and creation re
 sidency project developed through close engagement with one of Platform Da
 li's partner scientific institutions: the Barcelona Supercomputing Center 
 (BSC-CNS)\, ICFO (the Institute of Photonic Sciences)\, the Institute of M
 arine Sciences (ICM-CSIC)\, the Institute for High Energy Physics (IFAE)\,
  and the ecosystem of the Barcelona Biomedical Research Park (PRBB)\, incl
 uding the Centre for Genomic Regulation (CRG) and EMBL Barcelona. Five art
 ists are selected\, each hosted by one of the partner institutions. WHAT T
 HE PROGRAMME EXPECTS: research is treated as a creative practice in its ow
 n right\, and residents are explicitly not expected to arrive with a fully
  defined artwork. The residency is designed to support an initial phase of
  open-ended enquiry inside a scientific research environment\, letting que
 stions\, methods and ideas emerge through dialogue and experimentation\; t
 hat research phase then informs the development and production of a new ar
 twork\, so research and creation run as sequential\, interconnected stages
 . TIMELINE: open call launched 2 July 2026\; applications close 7 Septembe
 r 2026 at 23:59 CET\; selected artists notified by November 2026\; residen
 ts publicly announced 2 December 2026. Two residency periods are available
 \, February to April 2027 and September to November 2027\, with final date
 s agreed between Platform Dali\, the host scientific institution and each 
 resident. Full application criteria\, residency structure and terms and co
 nditions are on the open call page.\n\nOrganisation: Platform Dali\nLocati
 on: Barcelona\, Spain\, hosted at one of Platform Dali's partner scientifi
 c institutions. Open to artists of any nationality.\nAward: Artist fee of 
 EUR 7\,500 per selected artist\, plus access to a production budget of up 
 to EUR 25\,000 for the development of a new artwork. Five artists are sele
 cted. For artists based outside Barcelona\, Platform Dali covers travel\, 
 accommodation\, subsistence and travel insurance for both the introductory
  visit and the on-site residency\, within a predefined budget. Also provid
 ed: curatorial support from Platform Dali and the partner institution's te
 am throughout the residency\, and access to the host institution's facilit
 ies\, expertise and research communities\, subject to that institution's r
 egulations and operational requirements. For collectives\, the artist fee\
 , production budget and covered expenses are shared among members\, and a 
 maximum of two members may take part in the introductory visit and on-site
  residency.\nDuration: One of two residency periods: February to April 202
 7\, or September to November 2027. Final dates are agreed between Platform
  Dali\, the host institution and the resident.\n\nMore: https://platformda
 li.org/en/call/open-call-for-residencies-2027-what-is-real/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://platformdali.org/en/call/open-call-for-residencies-2027-what-is
 -real/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Barcelona\, Spain\, hosted at one of Platform Dali's partner scien
 tific institutions. Open to artists of any nationality.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:les-antipodes-numeric-2026-the-word@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: Numeric 2026: The Word (Les antipodes Research and Creati
 on Residency)
DESCRIPTION:Two research and creation residency grants in Agullana\, Catalo
 nia\, for artists\, thinkers and scientists of any branch (formal\, natura
 l\, social\, applied) working at the intersection of art\, culture\, scien
 ce and thought. THIS YEAR'S THEME\, The Word\, is squarely about large lan
 guage models. The framework moves from the word as creative principle acro
 ss traditions (the Judeo-Christian creating God\, the Quran as uncreated w
 ord\, the Sanskrit Om and the Vedas\, mantras that summon rather than desc
 ribe) to science relocating that intuition in the cell (Sagan's four-lette
 r alphabet of DNA as a material text\, Margulis countering that the langua
 ge of life is chemistry\, Witzany holding that all cell interactions are s
 ign-mediated)\, and then to LLMs\, which for the first time detach the wor
 d from the flesh and generate text through a probabilistic architecture tr
 ained on human language. The call asks what LLMs reveal about authorship\,
  originality and meaning when a system generates text that moves\, persuad
 es or deceives without experience or intention\, and whether we can still 
 say the models operate exclusively on human experience once they ingest th
 eir own output. It explicitly wants work that blurs comprehension and simu
 lation\, authorship and recombination\, and it names antecedents worth kno
 wing before applying: Ramon Llull's Ars Magna\, Joan Brossa's Sextina cibe
 rnetica\, Oulipo constraint writing\, oracles and divination\, oral-formul
 aic transmission\, translation as probabilistic approximation\, exquisite 
 corpses\, Burroughs's cut-ups\, automatic writing\, musical improvisation 
 and scientific simulation. Les antipodes asks for engagement 'beyond the i
 nstrumental use of the tools' and away from utopian and dystopian framings
 . WHAT YOU GET: spacious\, luminous\, independent work spaces plus a share
 d terrace in the centre of the village\, spaces for meeting\, dialogue and
  public presentation\, introductions to local\, academic\, artistic and pr
 ofessional contacts on request\, and support organising any activities tha
 t arise. The residency ends with an informal presentation\, and results ma
 y feed into the publications or public programme of Les antipodes. IMPORTA
 NTLY\, this is not an acquisition prize and involves no transfer of rights
 : authorship and intellectual property stay with the creators\, and select
 ion implies no acquisition of the work. Credits on resulting work must ack
 nowledge Numeric 2026 and its collaborators. WHO CAN APPLY: open to everyo
 ne of legal age\, any discipline or origin\, individually or as a collecti
 ve\, with the stipend shared among collective members. Applications must b
 e in Catalan or English whatever the working language of the project\, sub
 mitted through the online form with an optional PDF of up to 5 pages and 1
 0 MB. One application per person or collective. EVALUATION\, in order of w
 eight: singularity and innovation\; relevance to this year's LLM framework
 \; and interaction and transfer of value\, meaning relations with the soci
 al and geographical environment of Agullana and the Emporda and a plan for
  presenting the outcome in editorial\, on-site\, performative\, sound\, au
 diovisual or other form. The call may be declared void if no proposal reac
 hes the required level. PRACTICAL: residents must hold their own health co
 ver (EHIC for EU citizens\, or valid travel or health insurance for Spain 
 otherwise) and are responsible for their own equipment. Supported by the G
 eneralitat de Catalunya\, Agullana and Figueres town councils\, and Accion
  Cultural Espanola through the PICE national residencies programme. Terms 
 are published in Arabic\, Catalan\, English\, Spanish\, French and Chinese
 .\n\nOrganisation: Les antipodes\, with Bolit Centre d'Art Contemporani Gi
 rona and Museu de l'Emporda Figueres\nLocation: Agullana\, Alt Emporda\, C
 atalonia\, Spain. A village of 900 people\, 20 km from Figueres and the Vi
 lafant high-speed rail station\, 40 km from Perpignan\, 60 km from Girona\
 , 160 km from Barcelona.\nAward: Two grants. Each provides a stipend of EU
 R 1\,000 as taxable base (taxes and withholdings apply)\, round-trip trave
 l within the European Union\, and accommodation and meals for the four wee
 ks in Agullana. Travel and meals cover the resident only\, though the shar
 ed apartment can host immediate family by arrangement. Numeric also provid
 es the resources specified in the application and agreed during preparatio
 n. No application fee. Note that if a selected candidate lacks the profess
 ional registration needed to receive the stipend and payment has to be arr
 anged through Les antipodes\, the resulting costs are deducted from the st
 ipend.\nDuration: Four weeks\, 2 to 30 November 2026\, with both residenci
 es running simultaneously. Preceded by one month of preparation and follow
 ed by a two-month closing period. Applications 23 July to 8 September 2026
 \; decision announced 22 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://lesantipodes.com
 /numeric/2026_bases_en.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://lesantipodes.com/numeric/2026_bases_en.html?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Agullana\, Alt Emporda\, Catalonia\, Spain. A village of 900 peopl
 e\, 20 km from Figueres and the Vilafant high-speed rail station\, 40 km f
 rom Perpignan\, 60 km from Girona\, 160 km from Barcelona.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kultur-ensemble-palermo-residencies-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kultur Ensemble Palermo Residencies 2027 (Franco-German D
 uos)
DESCRIPTION:Kultur Ensemble Palermo\, the joint Franco-German cultural inst
 itute uniting the Institut francais Palermo and the Goethe-Institut Palerm
 o since 2021\, offers two three-month residencies promoting research and c
 reation on Franco-German projects with a close link to Palermo and Sicily.
  Residents work from the Bottega\, the 115 sq m artist's studio inaugurate
 d in October 2024 within the Cantieri Culturali alla Zisa. WHO CAN APPLY\,
  and this is the unusual constraint: applications must come from a FRANCO-
 GERMAN DUO\, meaning French or German nationality or working in France or 
 Germany\, and all applicants must be UNDER 36 at the time of the residency
 \, a requirement of the Franco-German Youth Office which funds the scheme.
  Beyond that\, applicants need a practice or project that genuinely justif
 ies the need for a studio\, and a project related to Palermo\, Sicily or t
 he Mediterranean region. COMMITMENTS: participate in at least two educatio
 nal activities such as a presentation of work or a workshop with Palermo s
 chools\; provide communication material for the institutes' social media a
 nd take part in a short video interview\; be free from other professional 
 commitments during the residency\; and hold civil liability insurance and 
 health insurance valid abroad for the full period. A public presentation e
 vent is NOT mandatory and can be discussed with the institutes during the 
 stay. The institutes also help establish contacts with the local cultural 
 network and institutions. APPLYING: all documents in a single PDF\, submit
 ted in duplicate\, one version in French or German and one in English\, to
  kulturensemblepalermo@gmail.com. Required: CV of maximum 2 pages per pers
 on for everyone involved including contact details and date of birth\; a p
 roject description with justification for the studio\, maximum 1 page\; an
 d a portfolio of maximum 10 pages total. An Italian-French-German jury of 
 experts decides at the end of September 2026\, with results announced by e
 mail and published on Kultur Ensemble Palermo's Instagram and the social c
 hannels of both institutes. NOTE ON DATES: the official English call PDF s
 ays 'two three-month residencies in 2026'\, which is an error carried into
  several republished versions. The residency periods listed in the same do
 cument\, and the French institute's own page\, both give 2027. Enquiries: 
 bianca.bozzeda@goethe.de (Goethe-Institut) or judith.testault@institutfran
 cais.it (Institut francais).\n\nOrganisation: Kultur Ensemble Palermo (Ins
 titut francais Palermo and Goethe-Institut Palermo)\, with the Franco-Germ
 an Youth Office (OFAJ/DFJW)\nLocation: Palermo\, Sicily. Studio at the Bot
 tega\, a 115 sq m space within the Cantieri Culturali alla Zisa\, shared w
 ith other residents.\nAward: Two residencies. Each provides a residency gr
 ant of EUR 1\,500 per person per month\, one return journey between home a
 nd Palermo capped at EUR 500 per person\, a budget of up to EUR 500 for re
 search\, creation and production costs (agreed in advance with the institu
 tes)\, and accommodation in the city with private rooms and shared common 
 spaces. Over three months that is EUR 4\,500 per person in grant alone\, a
 nd duos are funded per person. No application fee.\nDuration: Three months
 . Two slots in 2027: 11 January to 4 April\, and 20 September to 12 Decemb
 er. Deadline 6 September 2026 at midnight\; jury decision end of September
  2026.\n\nMore: https://www.institutfrancais.it/fr/palerme/bando-residenze
 -il-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.institutfrancais.it/fr/palerme/bando-residenze-il-2027?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Palermo\, Sicily. Studio at the Bottega\, a 115 sq m space within 
 the Cantieri Culturali alla Zisa\, shared with other residents.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:unidee-cittadellarte-ecosystems-living-communities-vol3@artificialnouve
 au.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ecosystems as Living Communities vol. III: UniCredit Grou
 p UNIDEE Connective Residencies 2026-27
DESCRIPTION:Third edition of Ecosystems as Living Communities\, supporting 
 two production residencies for emerging international artists working with
  socially engaged practices that address contemporary ecological issues. T
 WO PLACES\, and the split matters: one Italian artist and one internationa
 l artist residing in Europe. The framing follows the mission of Fondazione
  Pistoletto Cittadellarte\, the vision and work of Michelangelo Pistoletto
 \, and the UN Sustainable Development Goals\, and promotes interdisciplina
 ry collaboration across scientific\, technological and humanistic fields. 
 WHAT THEY WANT: projects that investigate ecosystems as interconnected com
 munities of humans and non-humans\, challenging anthropocentric perspectiv
 es and promoting an ecocentric mindset\, and that foster positive and rege
 nerative visions of desirable futures rather than only diagnosing crisis. 
 HOW IT RUNS: the two artists share a research phase including meetings wit
 h tutors\, researchers and experts as well as field visits\, so a collabor
 ative attitude and flexibility as the research evolves are described as es
 sential. Resulting works are publicly presented at Cittadellarte in Biella
  and disseminated online. IMPORTANT ON RIGHTS: the final artwork produced 
 during the residency BECOMES PART OF THE UNICREDIT ART COLLECTION\, so thi
 s is a production residency that ends in acquisition by a corporate collec
 tion\, not a no-strings creation grant. Weigh that against the fee before 
 applying. For large installations\, artists are also required to develop a
  simplified version of the work that can be installed in smaller exhibitio
 n spaces while preserving its conceptual integrity. APPLYING: complete the
  online form and submit the required documents\, all in English\, includin
 g a portfolio and CV as one PDF up to 5 MB containing a selected portfolio
  of maximum 5 projects or artworks most fitting to the call topic\, plus a
  brief biography and CV.\n\nOrganisation: Fondazione Pistoletto Cittadella
 rte and UNIDEE Residency Programs\, in collaboration with UniCredit Group\
 nLocation: Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy.\nAward: Two residencies. Each p
 rovides a gross artist fee of EUR 6\,500\, a production budget of up to EU
 R 2\,500 for costs directly related to producing the final artwork (instal
 lation supports\, equipment rental\, materials)\, round-trip travel and re
 search trips with a combined budget of EUR 1\,000 to 1\,500 per artist dep
 ending on distance\, accommodation in a single room with shared facilities
  at Cittadellarte plus a per diem of EUR 25 per day for up to 60 days\, an
 d a research budget of up to EUR 500 for fees to experts contributing to t
 he research. Cittadellarte separately covers technical support for the exh
 ibition installation\, project communication\, and a publication accompany
 ing the residency and final exhibition. No application fee.\nDuration: Res
 idencies take place between December 2026 and April 2027. The structure an
 d schedule are tailored to each project and defined collaboratively with t
 he artists. Deadline 6 September 2026 at 23:59\; shortlisted candidates ma
 y be interviewed online between late September and mid-October\; selection
  by mid-October 2026.\n\nMore: https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.ht
 ml?id=263&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.html?id=263&utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:unidee-cittadellarte-visiting-research-curator-2027-28@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: UNIDEE Residency Programs: Visiting Research Curator 2027
 -28
DESCRIPTION:Cittadellarte is recruiting a Visiting Research Curator to shap
 e the research arc of the UNIDEE Residency Modules for 2027-2028\, the rol
 e most recently held by Nina Fiocco and Gaia Martino. Founded by Michelang
 elo Pistoletto in 1999\, the residency has hosted thousands of artists and
  cultural professionals from 52 countries. WHAT THEY WANT: rather than a f
 ixed theme set in advance\, they want a curator who brings their own line 
 of inquiry and develops it collectively through the modules. Suggested the
 matic areas are the evolving landscape of socially engaged art practices\,
  research-driven and process-oriented practice\, and the conditions of cul
 tural work and cultural workers\, aligned with UNIDEE's focus on artistic 
 research in response to ecological\, geopolitical and technological challe
 nges. They also flag a specific interest in how contemporary art practices
  are archived\, drawing on the UNIDEE archive\, which documents over two d
 ecades of socially engaged art initiatives. THIS IS A JOB\, NOT A GRANT: t
 he duties list is long and operational. It covers proposing the overarchin
 g curatorial theme\, designing and delivering a minimum of 8 weekly reside
 ntial modules per year\, writing the curatorial statement and resident ope
 n call\, recruiting mentors and guests\, selecting residents\, hosting and
  facilitating every module in person\, communications and website texts\, 
 a dissemination strategy\, and conceiving and editing publications. There 
 is also teaching-adjacent work with the Unidee Academy of Contemporary Art
 s\, including public lectures and studio visits. Budget the time before ap
 plying. PROFILE: experience curating art in the public sphere and socially
  engaged art\, specialised knowledge of international contemporary art\, e
 xperience designing experimental and collaborative educational activities\
 , and experience with artistic research and dissemination standards. Excel
 lent English required\; Italian welcome but not essential. APPLYING: onlin
 e form with a project proposal of maximum 5000 characters stating your vis
 ion for the modules programme and a development strategy including possibl
 e mentors and guests\, plus a CV\, a portfolio of maximum three relevant p
 rojects\, and TWO LETTERS OF RECOMMENDATION\, so line up your referees ear
 ly. A jury of the UNIDEE Advisory Board\, the Director of Cittadellarte an
 d invited experts decides. They do not give feedback on unsuccessful propo
 sals.\n\nOrganisation: Fondazione Pistoletto Cittadellarte and UNIDEE Resi
 dency Programs\nLocation: Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy. Requires in-pers
 on presence at Cittadellarte for a minimum of one month plus the full leng
 th of each module.\nAward: Compensation and contractual terms commensurate
  with the candidate's level of professional experience\, starting from a g
 ross amount of EUR 22\,000 for the appointment\, excluding travel and hosp
 itality\, which are covered separately. No application fee.\nDuration: Tem
 porary appointment from November 2026 to December 2028\, covering the 2027
 -2028 research arc and a minimum of 8 residency modules per year. Deadline
  13 September 2026 at 23:59 CEST\; shortlisted candidates interviewed onli
 ne by mid-October 2026\, with the selected candidate announced a few days 
 later.\n\nMore: https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.html?id=269&utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.html?id=269&utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy. Requires in-person presence at Cit
 tadellarte for a minimum of one month plus the full length of each module.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:futures-of-listening-earth-immersion-trembling-planet-2027@artificialno
 uveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Futures of Listening x KNMI: Earth Immersion\, Trembling 
 Planet Residency
DESCRIPTION:One residency for an artist\, designer or maker working with so
 und\, exploring spatial sound in relation to seismology\, climate and disc
 harge-related acoustics\, run with the Royal Netherlands Meteorological In
 stitute. THE PREMISE: climate change is making major landslides\, ice-brea
 koff events from marine-terminating glaciers\, and sound-producing ice mov
 ements more common. These generate infrasonic vibrations inaudible to huma
 ns that sometimes spread across the entire planet and into the atmosphere.
  The residency asks how immersive sound techniques can make these events t
 angible and build meaningful sonic narratives from them. WHAT YOU GET ACCE
 SS TO: scientific data on seismological events\, vibrations\, infrasound a
 nd the movement of land and ice masses\, with scientific expertise from KN
 MI (lead researcher Jelle Assink) and artistic guidance from the Futures o
 f Listening team\, relating local events to global impact. RESEARCH QUESTI
 ONS to position your proposal against include how immersive sound can cult
 ivate situated listening to ecological processes and more-than-human encou
 nters\, how artistic and scientific methods of working with environmental 
 data can inform each other\, and what is gained\, lost or ethically at sta
 ke when environmental sounds are translated\, interpreted or reconstructed
  through spatial and synthetic means. DELIVERABLE: residents are expected 
 to create a final spatial sound production using the 4DSOUND system. Other
  outcomes may include immersive installations\, data-driven sonic works\, 
 audiovisual pieces\, software prototypes or reusable methodologies. Experi
 ence in art-science environments is welcome but not required\; you may ins
 tead explain why working in one would benefit your practice. HARD ELIGIBIL
 ITY GATE: applicants must be based in the Netherlands AND registered as an
  artist with the Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK). This is not negotiable a
 nd rules out non-NL applicants. Futures of Listening is a Netherlands-base
 d platform for spatial sound\, immersive experience\, performance and inst
 allation art\, founded by FIBER Festival\, 4DSOUND\, Robin Koek and Art of
  Sound at the Royal Conservatoire The Hague\, supported by CIIIC and Stimu
 leringsfonds.\n\nOrganisation: Futures of Listening (FIBER\, 4DSOUND\, Rob
 in Koek\, Art of Sound) in collaboration with KNMI\, the Royal Netherlands
  Meteorological Institute\nLocation: Partially self-hosted\, partially at 
 the 4DSOUND studio in Amsterdam\, and partially at KNMI in De Bilt near Ut
 recht\, Netherlands.\nAward: Total residency fee of EUR 7\,125\, calculate
 d as 25 commissioned days at a day rate of EUR 285. Six of those 25 days c
 an be spent at the 4DSOUND studio working with their team. Some additional
  financial support is available if needed\, including material and travel 
 costs\, but these must be clearly defined and agreed before the residency 
 starts. No application fee.\nDuration: January to March 2027\, structured 
 as 25 commissioned days. Deadline 13 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://fibe
 r.homerun.co/artist-residency-earth-immersion/en?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-earth-immersion/en?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Partially self-hosted\, partially at the 4DSOUND studio in Amsterd
 am\, and partially at KNMI in De Bilt near Utrecht\, Netherlands.
END:VEVENT
